    },
    {
      "claim": "L4/L5 records show essentially complete human intestinal absorption and high permeability with no net efflux for close basic-amine analogs: imipramine human Fa ~100% (C43,C48,C49,C53) and Caco-2 Papp ~10.8×10^-6 cm/s with efflux ratio ~1.1 (C58,C62,C63,C66), desipramine high absorption 95-100% (C60), amitriptyline freely soluble/high permeability (C45,C56), orphenadrine Fa 95% (C52), and chloroquine highly absorbed despite dicationic character (C44).",
      "card_ids": ["C43","C48","C49","C53","C58","C62","C63","C66","C60","C45","C56","C52","C44"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 hepatic-clearance records show imipramine and desipramine are extensively metabolized (human imipramine Clint 35.4 µL/min/mg, C77; literature 38-68, C71; desipramine human Clint 9.05 L/hr/kg, C68; higher rat/mouse values C67,C70,C74,C75,C76), indicating first-pass loss; however, these records do not report an oral F below 20% for these analogs and are not direct F outcomes, so they weaken but do not overturn the high prediction.",
      "card_ids": ["C77","C71","C68","C67","C70","C74","C75","C76"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Low-F records for selegiline (C02,C03,C06,C08,C09,C14,C16,C23), rotigotine/N-0437 (C10,C17,C25,C27), and brexanolone (C20) depend on specific liabilities (propargyl/MAO metabolism, phenolic conjugation/GI mucosal metabolism, steroid scaffold) absent in the query, so they do not affirmatively establish F<20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C02","C03","C06","C08","C09","C14","C16","C23","C10","C17","C25","C27","C20"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
